# What survives on a small team's rig: re-testing {{papers}} robot-learning papers with cheap hardware and little data

*Ravindu Somawansa, Shisso Robotics. Draft, not submitted.*

## Abstract

Most robot-learning papers are tested in simulation or on rigs that cost more than $20k; this is the case for {{rig_far}} of the {{papers}} papers we studied. We re-tested {{c_paper}} of their claims on a rig of about €{{rig_eur_round}}, an SO-101 arm with a Jetson Orin NX, with one protocol: the bar is fixed before the test, and the cheapest baseline, a signal we get for free, is run first on the same data. Of the {{b_n}} claims we could compare with such a baseline, {{b_lost}} lost to it; the clearest case is two world models that rank the action chunk the arm really executed worse than the actions alone do. Over all claims, {{c_paper_repro}} hold and {{t_c_auth}} fail with the authors' method on our data; {{t_c_other}} more fail only in our own variant of the method, because of our inference engine, or on a task outside the paper's regime. Many of these failures trace back to our small, short-task data as much as to the methods, and we list for each one what on our side may explain it. The code, the data and every number are public.

## 1. Introduction

Of the {{papers}} papers in this study, {{rig_far}} were shown in simulation or on a rig above $20k, and only {{rig_lt5k}} on a rig under $5k like ours. A small team that wants to use one of these methods has two options: trust the paper, or re-test the claim on its own hardware, which is slow and costly. The question we ask is whether a claim still holds for a small team, with cheap hardware, little data and short tasks.

We re-tested the claims one at a time on a single cheap rig, with the same protocol for each: we fix the bar before the test, and we first run the cheapest baseline, a signal we already have for free, on the same data. The baseline turned out to be the most useful part. LeWorldModel, for instance, predicts the next state of our arm far better than simply copying the last one, but it ranks the chunk the arm really executed worse than the actions alone do. Without the second baseline, we would have called it a success.

Over all the claims, {{c_paper_repro}} of {{c_paper}} hold. We split the failures by whose method failed: {{t_c_auth}} fail with the authors' method on our data, {{t_c_var}} fail in a variant of the method we had to build ourselves, {{t_c_eng}} fail because of our inference engine, and {{t_c_scope}} fails on a task outside the regime the paper targets. Section 5 looks at the baselines, Section 6 draws a testing order from the failures, and Section 7 asks, for each failure, how much comes from the method and how much from our data and setup. A failure on our rig is a statement about this rig and this data, not about the method on the hardware it was shown on.

## 2. Rig and data

The rig is an SO-101 arm (five joints and a gripper) with a top camera and a wrist camera. Inference runs on a Jetson Orin NX 16 GB, and training runs on rented cloud GPUs. The priced parts come to €{{rig_eur}}; with the {{rig_unpriced}} small parts not priced yet, we put the rig at about €{{rig_eur_round}}. All the cloud GPU time came to ${{cost}}.

We used four sets of data. The first is our own teleoperated demonstrations (`so101_bowls_all_trimmed`): {{tel_eps}} episodes and {{tel_frames}} frames at {{tel_fps}} fps, about {{tel_min}} minutes in total, on two short tasks where one bowl is placed into another. We split them into {{tel_train}} training and {{tel_val}} validation episodes, and used them to train and test the world models and the VLA policies. The second is rollouts of our trained policies on the arm (`so101-bowls-policy-rollouts`): {{ro_eps}} episodes, {{ro_succ}} successes and {{ro_fail}} failures, labelled by the operator, used for the failure monitors. Most monitor tests use a main set of {{ro_p_eps}} of these episodes ({{ro_p_succ}} successes, {{ro_p_fail}} failures), which leaves out the episodes of one policy family; FAIL-Detect and SAFE split the {{ro_succ}} successes in half, {{ro_half}} to calibrate and {{ro_half}} to test, over 20 random splits. A separate set of 88 of our own episodes, recorded with the top camera only, was used to check the reward model on our rig. The third set is ArmnetBench [@2607.24481], {{arm_n}} public labelled SO-101 rollouts from {{arm_pol}} policies, used for the reward models and judges. The last is {{tim_eps}} recorded episodes with {{tim_b}} chunk boundaries, used for the chunking and latency tests.

This data has clear limits. All tasks are short, about {{tel_ep_s}} s per episode, and none needs several steps or memory, so we say nothing about long-horizon tasks. There is one task family, one arm and one room. The outcome labels come from a single operator and were not given blind.

## 3. What we tested and how

We chose papers from the parts of the stack our rig runs, and they fall into four kinds (Table 1). World models ({{f_wm}} papers) learn a latent from the camera to predict the next state or to plan; we test whether the latent predicts or plans better than a signal we get for free. Failure monitors and judges ({{f_mon}} papers) say whether an episode is failing or how far it has progressed; we test whether they catch failures better than a trivial signal. VLA policies ({{f_vla}} papers) follow an instruction on the arm; we test whether they succeed on our arm. Chunking and edge inference ({{f_chunk}} papers) concern how a policy's action chunks are produced and joined; we test whether the joins are smoother and how long a chunk takes on the Orin.

Every test follows two rules. First, the bar is written before any result is seen, because a bar written after the result tends to drift toward it. The bars are ours: where the paper reports a number for the claim, the bar is set from it, and the register keeps that number next to ours. Second, before the method itself, we run the cheapest baseline of its kind on the same data. It is built from what we already have for free: the last frame, the joint values we send to the arm, the length of the episode, or the method with its main idea switched off. If the method loses to this baseline, its bar hardly matters.

Each claim gets one of four verdicts: it holds (the bar is met), it fails (the bar is missed), it is not proven (the test ran but cannot decide), or it is not tested yet. A paper fails if any of its claims fails. Because a failure can come from us rather than from the method, we split each failure by whose method failed: the authors' method run on our data, a variant we had to build because a part was not released or did not fit our rig, our inference engine, or a task outside the regime the paper targets. Two of the VLA tests are weaker than the rest: SmolVLA and VLA-JEPA were judged by the operator over 10 trials with no numeric bar fixed before, and 9 successes out of 10 is compatible with a true rate anywhere between {{vla_ci}} (Wilson score interval, 95 %).

Where we could, we also ran a control on our own conclusions. TurboVLA is the clearest case. We believed its architecture made our port of the policy ignore its instruction, and we fixed the bar first: if swapping the task moved the output by {{tv_bug}}, the bug was in our port; if by {{tv_data}}, the problem was the data. With the upstream trainer on our data, swapping the task moved the output by only {{tv_swap}}, while their released LIBERO checkpoint, run on our rig, moved it by {{tv_ctl}}. The problem was our data, not their architecture. We withdrew the claim, and it is not counted in this paper.

Table 1: The four kinds of papers, what we test in each, the cheapest baseline we build for it, and the verdicts.

| kind of paper | papers | what we test | cheapest baseline | verdicts |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| world models | {{f_wm}} | predict the next state; rank or plan actions | copy-last (next state = current state); the commanded joints or the actions alone | {{f_wm_v}} |
| failure monitors and judges | {{f_mon}} | catch failures; track progress | episode length; the same model with shuffled actions | {{f_mon_v}} |
| VLA policies | {{f_vla}} | succeed on our arm | none: success over 10 trials judged by the operator, no numeric bar fixed before | {{f_vla_v}} |
| chunking and edge inference | {{f_chunk}} | smoother joins between chunks; time per chunk | the same policy without the trick; the real-time budget on the Orin | {{f_chunk_v}} |

## 4. Results

Table 2 and Figure 1 give the verdicts, and Table 1 breaks them down by kind of paper. Of the {{c_paper}} claims, {{c_paper_repro}} hold, {{c_not_proven}} are not proven and {{c_not_tested}} are not tested yet. Of the {{c_paper_fail}} that fail, {{t_c_auth}} fail with the authors' method on our data, {{t_c_var}} in our own variant, {{t_c_eng}} because of our engine and {{t_c_scope}} on a task outside the paper's regime. Counted by paper, {{p_reproduces}} of the {{papers}} reproduce, and {{t_p_auth}} fail with the authors' method. D-JEPA is counted as not tested: we ran it, but only with proxy labels, not with the success labels the paper uses.

The pattern by kind looks sharp, with all {{f_mon}} failure monitors and judges failing and all {{f_vla}} VLA policies reproducing, but neither half is strong evidence. Most of the monitor failures are in our own variants, and two of the three VLA results rest on 10 trials with no numeric bar. The appendix gives every paper with its claim, its bar and our result.

