## 5. The cheapest baseline

The cheapest baseline is the signal we get for free on the same data as the method (Table 1). It needs no training and runs in minutes. We compared {{b_n}} paper claims with such a baseline (Table 3). The method clearly beat it in {{b_beat}} cases, beat it by less than its bar in {{b_under}}, and lost to it in {{b_lost}}. The losses are not equally strong. LeWorldModel and DINO-WM are the clearest, but they are the same test on the same data, run on two models. ContactGuard's loss is in our variant, Robometer's comes from how the dataset ends its episodes (below), and adaptive chunking's 0.973 against 0.940 has no interval. A paired bootstrap over episodes for all {{b_n}} rows is still to be run; until then, the count of losses should be read with this in mind.

The choice of baseline decides the verdict, even for a single model. LeWorldModel predicts the next latent far better than copy-last, with an error of {{b_lewm_pred}} against {{b_lewm_cl}}, and that claim holds. Yet the same model ranks the chunk that was really executed worse than the actions alone do, {{ex_c_ours}} against {{ex_c_base}}, and that claim fails. Predicting well is not the same as planning well, and testing only one of the two would give the wrong picture.

The same baseline is also not equally strong on all data. On ArmnetBench, episode length beats the reward model ({{ex_b_len}} against {{ex_b_ours}}), because there a failed episode tends to run until the time limit, so the length gives the label away. On our own 88 episodes, where failures do not run to the time limit, length is close to chance ({{b_len_own}}). What a judge must beat therefore depends on who stopped the episode, and the baseline has to be computed on one's own data.

Copy-last and the joints are strong partly because our task is short and simple: little moves apart from the arm, and the arm's state is already in the joints. On long tasks with many moving objects a learned latent has more to add, and we did not test that. Some papers report a cheap baseline themselves (shuffled actions for ReAP, a random pick for PACT-VLA, net joint displacement for HWM), and on our data those methods beat it too; Robometer reports no episode-length baseline. In practice, the baseline is the first thing to run: if the method loses to it, one can stop there, and only if it wins is the bar worth checking.

Table 3: The paper claims we compared with a cheap baseline, on the same data.

| method | baseline (what is measured) | method | baseline | outcome |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| LeWorldModel | copy-last (latent error at 30 steps, lower is better) | 0.31 | 0.89 | beat |
| LeWorldModel | actions alone (executed chunk ranked first) | 0.60 | 0.68–0.90 | lost |
| DINO-WM | actions alone (executed chunk ranked first) | 0.61 | 0.68–0.90 | lost |
| RoboJEPA | copy-last (9-step latent error) | 11.8 % below copy-last | – | beat, under its bar of 15 % |
| ContactGuard | the current latent (failure AUROC) | 0.70 | 0.82 | lost |
| ReAP | shuffled actions (failure AUROC) | 0.789 | 0.708 | beat |
| Robometer | episode length (failure AUROC, ArmnetBench) | 0.818 | 0.938 | lost |
| HWM | net joint displacement (cosine to the macro-action) | 0.608 | 0.551 | beat |
| Adaptive chunking | fixed chunk length (boundary jump, lower is better) | 0.973 | 0.940 | lost (no interval) |
| PACT-VLA | a random pick (error of the executed window) | −0.27° against random | – | beat |
| REMORA | per-frame readout (readout flips) | 0.70 × per-frame | – | beat, under its bar of 0.5 × |

## 6. A testing order

The failures point to three checks, in this order. First, time the method on the target device: adaptive chunking needs {{ex_a_ours}} for one decision against a budget of {{ex_a_bar}}, partly because our engine samples without batching, and this is the cheapest failure to find. Second, compare it with the cheapest baseline on one's own data: this is where the world models fail, since the commanded joints already hold the arm's state on a short task, and it is also where a dataset can mislead, as episode length does on ArmnetBench. Only then is it worth checking the method against its bar. Of the three, only the learned-latent pattern rests on more than one case, so we offer this as a working order, not as a taxonomy of how methods fail.

## 7. Why they failed here: the method, our data, or our setup

For each failure, Table 4 says whose method failed and what on our side may explain it. Most failures have at least one such cause, so a failure here is a failure on this rig, with this data, and not a verdict on the method in general.

Our data is small and simple. We have {{tel_train}} training episodes of one short task, about {{tel_ep_s}} s each, where the papers use hours of varied data; HWM, for instance, uses about 130 h against our 0.6 h. On a short task with one arm, the commanded joints already hold most of the state, which leaves little for a latent to add. The task is also easy for our policy, since SmolVLA is at its ceiling on it, so there is little uncertainty for an entropy rule to read: the chunk length chosen by adaptive chunking sat at its minimum in 86 % of decisions. For H-JEPA, our task lies outside the regime the paper targets, as there is no gap between slow and fast parts of the scene, so we count its failure as out of scope rather than a failure of the method.

We also have too few failures. Our policies mostly succeed, and the main rollout set has only {{ro_p_fail}} failures in {{ro_p_eps}} episodes. A bar such as five failures caught out of eight is coarse, and a single episode can change the verdict (ReAP, VLA-Corrector, SURVEYOR).

In {{t_p_var}} papers our version of the method is not exactly theirs. For ContactGuard we used mixed policies and two cameras, where the paper uses one policy and three cameras. For FAIL-Detect and SAFE we used our world model's surprise as the score instead of their own scores. ReAP's risk head ran on our world model's latent, and our VLA-Corrector verifier works on pooled image statistics instead of their 54 features with the arm state. The drafter of SURVEYOR [@surveyor2026] is not released, and REMORA's progress atoms are written by hand. These are failures of our variants, and we do not claim that the authors' methods fail.

Our inference engine matters as well. vla.cpp samples sequentially and has no batching, so adaptive chunking pays for 20 full passes, while the paper's 106 ms was measured with batching on an A800. For PACT-VLA, the time of the check is projected rather than measured. Finally, several tests were offline only: planning ranked recorded chunks, and chunking measured smoothness on recorded episodes. None of these ran in closed loop on the arm.

Table 4: Each failed or not-proven paper, whose method failed, what we measured, and what on our side may explain it.

| failed paper | whose method | our result | what may be on our side |
|---|---|---|---|
| LeWorldModel | authors' | planning rank 0.60, action-only 0.68–0.90 | one short task, 157 episodes, one seed; offline ranking only |
| DINO-WM | authors' | planning rank 0.61, action-only 0.68–0.90 | same data as LeWorldModel; one training |
| RoboJEPA | authors' | 11.8 % below copy-last, bar 15 % | near miss; the 1B model reached 15.8 % on 30 clips only |
| H-JEPA | outside its regime | progress ρ −0.73, bar −0.80; false stalls 52 % | our task is outside the paper's regime |
| HWM | not proven | not proven: high level worse at 2 s | 0.6 h of one task against their 130 h |
| ContactGuard | our variant | AUROC 0.70, current latent 0.82 | mixed policies and 2 cameras against one policy and 3 cameras |
| FAIL-Detect and SAFE | our variant | 22.7 % of successes flagged at α 5 % | our score, not theirs; 22 successes for calibration |
| ReAP | our variant | 3 of 8 failures caught | their risk head on our world model's latent, not their features; 8 failures |
| VLA-Corrector | our variant | 2 of 8, AUROC 0.59 | our features, not their 54; 8 failures |
| Robometer | authors' | AUROC 0.818, length 0.938 | zero-shot only; ArmnetBench failures run to the time limit (on our rollouts length is near chance) |
| SURVEYOR | our variant | threshold 1.249 against 0.20; 3 of 8 | their drafter not released; 8 failures |
| REMORA | our variant | readout flips 0.70 of the per-frame readout | hand-written progress atoms |
| Adaptive chunking | authors' (latency: our engine) | 4,751 ms per decision; length 2 in transport and at the grasp | sequential engine, no batching; task at ceiling for our policy |
| PACT-VLA | our engine | 37 ticks of delay against 8 | check time projected; sequential engine |
| Real-Time Chunking | not proven | not proven: 71–73 % smoother, no arm run | offline smoothness only |
| Horizon × depth | not proven | not proven: 0.78, bar 0.70 | one policy, one task; arm test not run |

## 8. Limitations

Our data covers one short task family, about {{tel_ep_s}} s per episode, with one arm, two cameras and about {{tel_min}} minutes of demonstrations, so it says nothing about long-horizon tasks or varied scenes. There are few failures to test the monitors on, {{ro_p_fail}} in the main set of {{ro_p_eps}}. Many methods are our own variants rather than the authors' code end to end (Table 4), and the planning and chunking tests ran offline, never in closed loop on the arm. A single operator ran the arm and labelled the outcomes without being blind to them, many tests use small samples and a single training seed, and few results come with a confidence interval; a bootstrap over episodes for the baseline comparisons is still to be run. Two VLA results have no numeric bar and rest on 10 trials. The bars are ours, not the papers'. We chose the papers from the stack we run, so the rates describe these papers and not the field. Finally, our own mistakes are possible, as the TurboVLA case shows (Section 3).

## 9. Related work

Two works evaluate policies on the same arm: a real-world VLA benchmark with a taxonomy of failures and recoveries [@2606.08881], and ArmnetBench, a fleet of SO-101 cells that evaluates policies at scale [@2607.24481]. In simulation, vla-eval runs VLAs across 14 benchmarks [@2603.13966], and another benchmark studies the quality of demonstrations [@2610.18869]. These works rank policies. We instead take the claim a paper makes about its method and test it against a bar fixed in advance, on one cheap rig. The closest work is RoboSeed [@2610.40026], which scores VLA and world-action models on five dimensions, including reproduction evidence and deployment affordability; its authors note that the score is not a probability that a result is correct. We give one verdict per claim on one rig, with the data behind it and the reason we think it failed.

## 10. Conclusion

We re-tested {{c_paper}} claims from {{papers}} robot-learning papers on a rig of about €{{rig_eur_round}}, with under ${{cost_round}} of cloud GPU. {{c_paper_repro}} held, and {{t_c_auth}} failed with the authors' method on our data. The most useful part was the cheapest baseline: the same world model could pass one baseline and fail another, and {{b_lost}} of the {{b_n}} methods we compared with one lost to a signal we had for free, although only two of these losses are clear so far. Our advice to anyone re-testing a paper on a cheap rig is to run that baseline first, on their own data, with the bar fixed before the test. Everything is public, and every number can be rebuilt from the repository. Authors who think a failure here is ours (Table 4) are welcome to tell us, and we will correct it.

